{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qu9b77FQsn6hsWeLGZm7jv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\paragraph{Model scale and architecture.}\n2\tOur primary experiments span 7--14B parameters: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct (\\S\\ref{sec:scale-14b}). Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at substantially larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested. Preliminary cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \\S\\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\\ref{app:cross-arch}) confirms that the monotonic phase gradient extends beyond the Qwen2 architecture ($n{=}250$, $d_z{=}1.12$ at $s{=}0.25$; all tested conditions $p_{\\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-37}$) but with substantially reduced interaction magnitude (${\\sim}25{\\times}$ smaller than Qwen2-architecture models). The pattern has been tested on two architecture families (Qwen2ForCausalLM, LlamaForCausalLM) at two scale points (7B, 14B); comprehensive validation across more diverse architectures (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention) and larger scales is needed for full generalization claims.\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Task coverage.}\n5\tWe evaluate on three benchmarks spanning mathematical and scientific reasoning: MATH-500 (primary), GSM8K (\\S\\ref{sec:cross-benchmark}), and GPQA Diamond (\\S\\ref{sec:cross-benchmark-gpqa}). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K supports the phase gradient direction under importance-based eviction but reveals that random eviction is non-significant (CIs spanning zero), precluding meaningful ratio computation. On MATH-500, strategy specificity is quantifiable ($15$--$17{\\times}$); on GSM8K, it manifests as a qualitative gap (significant vs.\\ non-significant). This suggests that the phase-strategy interaction is robust in direction but task-dependent in form---complex reasoning (MATH-500) produces quantitative strategy contrast while simpler arithmetic (GSM8K) produces qualitative separation. Qwen GSM8K outputs were truncated at \\texttt{max\\_new\\_tokens}${=}$2047, so Qwen GSM8K absolute values carry this limitation; DeepSeek GSM8K data is untruncated. Our task-complexity explanation for specificity attenuation (lower cognitive load $\\to$ more uniform attention $\\to$ reduced strategy sensitivity) is supported by within-benchmark correlational evidence but requires validation across a broader range of task complexities; whether different reasoning tasks---code generation, multi-hop QA---produce yet different attenuation profiles remains open. Random eviction on GSM8K exhibits numerical instability (extreme outlier II values), likely a systematic interaction between reduced-precision arithmetic and score-independent eviction.\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Eviction strategy scope and H$_2$O confound.}\n8\tOur importance-based eviction uses cumulative attention scores (H$_2$O-style scoring~\\cite{h2o-neurips2023}, Eq.~\\ref{eq:kv-importance}), which creates an inherent confound: head pruning alters the attention distribution from which importance scores are computed, and eviction then operates on these degraded scores. The compound interaction patterns we report---including importance discrimination collapse and the phase-strategy crossover---may be partly attributable to this circular dependency rather than to compound sparsity \\emph{per se}. Other attention-mass-based methods (SnapKV~\\cite{snapkv-neurips2024}, PyramidKV~\\cite{pyramidkv-colm2025}) share the same scoring basis and would likely exhibit a similar confound, whereas strategies that do not derive scores from the pruned attention distribution (e.g., learned eviction policies, recency-based heuristics) may exhibit qualitatively different compound interaction dynamics. Disentangling the contribution of score degradation from the intrinsic compound interaction requires testing eviction methods whose scoring is independent of head pruning; this is an important next step.\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Symmetric sparsity assumption.}\n11\tOur analysis applies symmetric sparsity levels: the same fraction $s$ is used for both head pruning and KV cache eviction. A preliminary spot check under asymmetric allocation (Appendix~\\ref{app:asymmetric-robustness}) supports that the phase gradient is robust to head/KV budget ratios, but systematic characterization of the full asymmetric interaction surface remains future work.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Phase segmentation validation.}\n14\tThe \\texttt{<think>} token validation of sparsity-based phase segmentation (\\S\\ref{sec:background}, Appendix~\\ref{app:think-validation}) is limited to a single model (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B) on a single benchmark (GSM8K, $n{=}48$). Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct does not generate explicit reasoning markers, precluding the same validation. Whether the high agreement ($\\kappa{=}0.88$) generalizes to other models with explicit CoT delimiters, or to non-mathematical reasoning tasks, remains open.\n15\t\n16\t\\paragraph{Mechanism.}\n17\tThe importance discrimination collapse mechanism is a correlational marker, not a complete causal explanation: both strategies exhibit near-identical Gini drops yet severity diverges by $15$--$17{\\times}$, indicating that Gini collapse alone is insufficient. Causal validation through controlled entropy manipulation is needed. The entropy redistribution component replicates on only 2/4 Qwen conditions, suggesting model-specific factors.\n18\t\n19\t\\paragraph{Perplexity vs.\\ accuracy.}\n20\tOur primary severity metric is perplexity-based (II), chosen because its per-token granularity enables the phase-level decomposition central to our analysis; accuracy is binary per-problem and cannot be meaningfully decomposed by reasoning phase. Accuracy validation on MATH-500 (\\S\\ref{sec:accuracy-validation}, Appendix~\\ref{app:accuracy-details}) shows gradual decline through $s{=}0.25$ ($\\delta{=}-4$ to $-13$\\,pp) followed by a sharp cliff at $s{=}0.30$ ($\\delta{=}-27$\\,pp) for Qwen ($n{=}100$, truncation $\\leq 4\\%$). DeepSeek accuracy deltas are confounded by rising truncation ($16\\%$ to $76\\%$); non-truncated analysis reveals a dual-regime structure but is limited by small surviving samples at high sparsity. Comprehensive accuracy evaluation across benchmarks remains an important next step.\n21\t\n22\t\\paragraph{Sparsity range.}\n23\tOur experiments span $s \\in \\{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\\}$, sufficient to establish monotonicity at the tested levels (JT test $p \\ll 10^{-10}$) but insufficient to characterize the functional form of the auxiliary ratio $G(s)$ or identify potential saturation thresholds. More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics. We do not report inference throughput or latency measurements, which are necessary for a complete deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization. Extended limitations in Appendix~\\ref{app:limitations}.\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MEt44HPbFSnzxSP94rba8J","session_id":"f803c3f7-d0f1-4587-9cc5-4b6689d0c433","uuid":"bbb3bfca-ee89-4d0d-b178-3503fc03c26e","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:58:01.143Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019gZwYyyLaT8Q1gcpszCFdo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U3V1gU9PGXTEwLPxg1xDvm","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\nScoring uses three dimensions:\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=completely sound, 4=mostly sound minor issues, 3=adequate with concerns, 2=significant issues, 1=fundamentally flawed\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=highly exciting, 4=exciting, 3=somewhat, 2=not exciting, 1=not at all\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5, half-point increments): 5=award-worthy (top 2.5%), 4-4.5=strong accept, 3-3.5=accept/borderline accept, 2-2.5=borderline reject, 1-1.5=reject\n- **Confidence** (1-5): your expertise level\n- EMNLP acceptance rate ~22%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity Severity in Reasoning LLMs\"\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) severity is phase-modulated: compound interaction amplifies monotonically across reasoning phases (paired median diff 0.39→8.48, Spearman ρ=1.0, n=500). Universal across 3 models, 2 architectures (Qwen2, LlamaForCausalLM), 2 math benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K). Strategy modulation is architecture-dependent (Qwen2 16.6× imp/rand ratio vs LLaMA 2.4×). Discrimination collapse (Gini/CV decline) identified as mechanism.\n\n**Three contributions:**\n1. Phase gradient universality — compound interaction severity increases monotonically from high→low sparsity reasoning phases, dz≈1.0-1.2 across all models, ρ=1.0\n2. Architecture-dependent strategy modulation — importance vs random eviction separation strong on Qwen2 (16.6×), weak on LLaMA (2.4×, 1/4 Bonferroni sig)\n3. Discrimination collapse mechanism — Gini/CV decline under head pruning, cross-model consistent\n\n**Related Work (§2):**\n- Single-axis KV cache: H2O [3], StreamingLLM [4], Ada-KV [2], ThinKV [5] (phase-aware but single-axis), R-KV [6]\n- Compound compression: SparK [1] (joint pruning+eviction, but assumes uniform interaction)\n- Importance score dynamics: HIES [7] (score collapse in single-axis)\n- Phase transitions: Physics of KV Cache Compression [8] (compression-rate phase transitions, orthogonal concept)\n- Adaptive eviction: Lethe [9] (layer-time-adaptive, complementary)\n\n**Methods:**\n- Interaction Index (II) = L_compound - (L_prune + L_evict - L_dense)\n- Quantile-based 3-phase segmentation of reasoning chains using dense model's attention sparsity\n- Per-problem paired design, Wilcoxon + Bonferroni, Cohen's dz, bootstrap CIs\n\n**Experimental scope:**\n- 3 models (DS-Qwen-7B, QW-7B, DS-Llama-8B), 2 architectures, 2 math benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K), 1 negative result benchmark (GPQA)\n- Sparsity levels: s015-s030, strategies: importance/random\n- GPQA: 0/10 significant, non-monotonic → scope limited to math reasoning\n- Pilot mitigation: 18% recovery, below 30% gate\n\n**Key tables:**\n- Table 1: MATH-500 importance phase gradient, both models ρ=1.0\n- Table 2: LLaMA cross-arch validation, dz≈1.0\n- Table 3: Strategy specificity by architecture\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark comparison (MATH > GSM8K >> GPQA)\n- Table 6 (appendix): GPQA detailed null results\n\n**References:** 9 total citations. Only 2 are from peer-reviewed venues (H2O at NeurIPS'23, StreamingLLM at ICLR'24, Lethe at AAAI'26). The rest are arXiv preprints.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### A. Novelty Assessment\n1. How novel is the core observation (phase-dependent compound interaction)? Has anyone studied this before?\n2. How novel is the Interaction Index metric? Is it just standard super-additivity decomposition?\n3. How novel is the phase segmentation approach? How does it differ from ThinKV's phase-aware approach?\n4. Is \"discrimination collapse\" a genuinely new mechanistic insight vs. restating HIES's finding?\n5. What is the overall contribution level — new phenomenon discovery, incremental extension, or confirmation study?\n6. Given that the phenomenon only holds for math reasoning (GPQA fails), how does this affect novelty claims?\n\n### B. Related Work Assessment\n1. Is the related work comprehensive? What major lines of work are missing?\n2. Are the following adequately discussed:\n   - Structured pruning literature (not just attention head pruning)\n   - Chain-of-thought analysis literature (reasoning phase analysis)\n   - Other compound compression works beyond SparK\n   - Theoretical understanding of compression (rate-distortion, etc.)\n3. With only 9 references, is the bibliography too sparse for EMNLP?\n4. Is the positioning against SparK [1] fair? Does SparK really assume \"uniformly super-additive\"?\n5. Is the differentiation from ThinKV [5] clear? ThinKV already does phase-aware KV compression — what exactly is new here?\n6. Is the relationship to HIES [7] properly characterized?\n\n### C. Scope and Framing\n1. Title says \"Reasoning LLMs\" but results only hold for mathematical reasoning — is this overclaiming?\n2. Is the paper primarily an empirical finding paper or a methods paper? Is the contribution level appropriate for EMNLP main?\n3. How actionable are the findings? The pilot mitigation failed the pre-registered gate — does this undermine practical relevance?\n4. Is the paper better suited for Findings vs. main conference?\n\n### D. Detailed Scoring\nProvide scores with justification for: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment, Confidence.\n\n### E. Summary\n- 3 strengths (bullet points)\n- 3+ weaknesses (bullet points)\n- Questions for authors (numbered)\n- Suggestions for improving positioning/framing\n\nWrite your review in full detail. Be rigorous but fair."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1197,"cache_read_input_tokens":58051,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1197},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f803c3f7-d0f1-4587-9cc5-4b6689d0c433","uuid":"6abd765e-d936-4431-97b7-5e614920ffb4"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a420d25a0e20dc013","tool_use_id":"toolu_01U3V1gU9PGXTEwLPxg1xDvm","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\nScoring uses three dimensions:\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=completely sound, 4=mostly sound minor issues, 3=adequate with concerns, 2=significant issues, 1=fundamentally flawed\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=highly exciting, 4=exciting, 3=somewhat, 2=not exciting, 1=not at all\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5, half-point increments): 5=award-worthy (top 2.5%), 4-4.5=strong accept, 3-3.5=accept/borderline accept, 2-2.5=borderline reject, 1-1.5=reject\n- **Confidence** (1-5): your expertise level\n- EMNLP acceptance rate ~22%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity Severity in Reasoning LLMs\"\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) severity is phase-modulated: compound interaction amplifies monotonically across reasoning phases (paired median diff 0.39→8.48, Spearman ρ=1.0, n=500). Universal across 3 models, 2 architectures (Qwen2, LlamaForCausalLM), 2 math benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K). Strategy modulation is architecture-dependent (Qwen2 16.6× imp/rand ratio vs LLaMA 2.4×). Discrimination collapse (Gini/CV decline) identified as mechanism.\n\n**Three contributions:**\n1. Phase gradient universality — compound interaction severity increases monotonically from high→low sparsity reasoning phases, dz≈1.0-1.2 across all models, ρ=1.0\n2. Architecture-dependent strategy modulation — importance vs random eviction separation strong on Qwen2 (16.6×), weak on LLaMA (2.4×, 1/4 Bonferroni sig)\n3. Discrimination collapse mechanism — Gini/CV decline under head pruning, cross-model consistent\n\n**Related Work (§2):**\n- Single-axis KV cache: H2O [3], StreamingLLM [4], Ada-KV [2], ThinKV [5] (phase-aware but single-axis), R-KV [6]\n- Compound compression: SparK [1] (joint pruning+eviction, but assumes uniform interaction)\n- Importance score dynamics: HIES [7] (score collapse in single-axis)\n- Phase transitions: Physics of KV Cache Compression [8] (compression-rate phase transitions, orthogonal concept)\n- Adaptive eviction: Lethe [9] (layer-time-adaptive, complementary)\n\n**Methods:**\n- Interaction Index (II) = L_compound - (L_prune + L_evict - L_dense)\n- Quantile-based 3-phase segmentation of reasoning chains using dense model's attention sparsity\n- Per-problem paired design, Wilcoxon + Bonferroni, Cohen's dz, bootstrap CIs\n\n**Experimental scope:**\n- 3 models (DS-Qwen-7B, QW-7B, DS-Llama-8B), 2 architectures, 2 math benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K), 1 negative result benchmark (GPQA)\n- Sparsity levels: s015-s030, strategies: importance/random\n- GPQA: 0/10 significant, non-monotonic → scope limited to math reasoning\n- Pilot mitigation: 18% recovery, below 30% gate\n\n**Key tables:**\n- Table 1: MATH-500 importance phase gradient, both models ρ=1.0\n- Table 2: LLaMA cross-arch validation, dz≈1.0\n- Table 3: Strategy specificity by architecture\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark comparison (MATH > GSM8K >> GPQA)\n- Table 6 (appendix): GPQA detailed null results\n\n**References:** 9 total citations. Only 2 are from peer-reviewed venues (H2O at NeurIPS'23, StreamingLLM at ICLR'24, Lethe at AAAI'26). The rest are arXiv preprints.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### A. Novelty Assessment\n1. How novel is the core observation (phase-dependent compound interaction)? Has anyone studied this before?\n2. How novel is the Interaction Index metric? Is it just standard super-additivity decomposition?\n3. How novel is the phase segmentation approach? How does it differ from ThinKV's phase-aware approach?\n4. Is \"discrimination collapse\" a genuinely new mechanistic insight vs. restating HIES's finding?\n5. What is the overall contribution level — new phenomenon discovery, incremental extension, or confirmation study?\n6. Given that the phenomenon only holds for math reasoning (GPQA fails), how does this affect novelty claims?\n\n### B. Related Work Assessment\n1. Is the related work comprehensive? What major lines of work are missing?\n2. Are the following adequately discussed:\n   - Structured pruning literature (not just attention head pruning)\n   - Chain-of-thought analysis literature (reasoning phase analysis)\n   - Other compound compression works beyond SparK\n   - Theoretical understanding of compression (rate-distortion, etc.)\n3. With only 9 references, is the bibliography too sparse for EMNLP?\n4. Is the positioning against SparK [1] fair? Does SparK really assume \"uniformly super-additive\"?\n5. Is the differentiation from ThinKV [5] clear? ThinKV already does phase-aware KV compression — what exactly is new here?\n6. Is the relationship to HIES [7] properly characterized?\n\n### C. Scope and Framing\n1. Title says \"Reasoning LLMs\" but results only hold for mathematical reasoning — is this overclaiming?\n2. Is the paper primarily an empirical finding paper or a methods paper? Is the contribution level appropriate for EMNLP main?\n3. How actionable are the findings? The pilot mitigation failed the pre-registered gate — does this undermine practical relevance?\n4. Is the paper better suited for Findings vs. main conference?\n\n### D. Detailed Scoring\nProvide scores with justification for: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment, Confidence.\n\n### E. Summary\n- 3 strengths (bullet points)\n- 3+ weaknesses (bullet points)\n- Questions for authors (numbered)\n- Suggestions for improving positioning/framing\n\nWrite your review in full detail. Be rigorous but fair.","uuid":"0b7b3c94-6c58-410e-b880-9fe592a5fae3","session_id":"f803c3f7-d0f1-4587-9cc5-4b6689d0c433"}
