ight have been in earlier drafts or the metadata.\n\nNow I'm moving into the roundtable synthesis, starting with identifying where the reviewers converge and diverge. Both gave identical overall and excitement scores of 3.0/5, and both flagged the circular dependency confound as a critical issue.\n\nThey also align on the correlational nature of the discrimination findings, the limited generalizability to math reasoning only, the overclaimed title, the failed mitigation pilot, and they both recognize the statistical rigor and transparency as strengths. Where they diverge is on phase segmentation validation, novelty assessment, and some technical concerns — R1 worries about generation-time confounds between dense and sparse models that R2 doesn't mention, while R2 engages more with the CoT literature gap and notes the trivial Spearman correlation on just 4 data points.\n\nI'm noticing both reviewers cited content that isn't actually in the paper, so I need to account for that when reconciling their scores. The consensus is that this lands at a 3.0 overall — borderline for Findings rather than the main conference — with the phase gradient universality holding up as a valid contribution within its narrower scope than advertised, though the mechanism remains incomplete and correlational. Before finalizing the consolidated review, I should double-check R1's specific concern about the head pruning ratio.\n\nLooking at the paper, I see head pruning is mentioned at ratio p in Section 3.1, with sparsity values specified for KV cache eviction, but I'm having trouble locating where p itself is actually defined. R1 suggested p equals s (symmetric compound sparsity), yet I can't pinpoint that statement in the visible text. The interaction metric formula is there, but the parameter specification seems to be missing or unclear. The paper introduces p as a parameter for head pruning ratio but never actually specifies its value—only the KV eviction sparsity levels s are given. This is a genuine methodological gap that needs to be addressed in the final report. Now I'm reviewing the reference list to check the citations and their publication details.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12274,"cache_read_input_tokens":59248,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12274},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f803c3f7-d0f1-4587-9cc5-4b6689d0c433","uuid":"1e7507a9-98e2-46e5-b65a-178bb16ba1a1"}
